# Conditional Style Function Pattern

When styles are conditional, use a style function that:
1) selects variant styles with a `switch` or an object map, and
2) returns one grouped style object consumed once in the component.

- Put conditional branching inside the style function, not inline in JSX.
- Use a `switch` or variant object map for variants/states (for example theme, intent, size, status).
- Build and return a single object that groups related classes with simple keys (for example `container`, `title`, `description`, `cta`).
- Hoist static `base` and `styles` objects to module scope so they are created once.
- Keep the style function lightweight: select an existing variant and apply fallback handling only.
- In the component, call the style function one time (for example `const styles = getStyles(...)`) and reference its keys in JSX.
- Keep all variant-related styles together in that object so updates happen in one place.
- For medium/large components, extract style constants and selector into a colocated style file (for example `ComponentNameStyles.ts`) and import the selector.

## Example (Object Map)

```tsx
type Variant = 'default' | 'highlight' | 'muted';
type StyleGroup = {
  container: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  cta: string;
};

const base: StyleGroup = {
  container: 'rounded-md border p-4',
  title: 'text-sm font-semibold',
  description: 'text-sm',
  cta: 'text-sm underline',
};

const styles: Record<Variant, StyleGroup> = {
  default: {
    container: `${base.container} border-gray-300 bg-white`,
    title: `${base.title} text-gray-900`,
    description: `${base.description} text-gray-700`,
    cta: `${base.cta} text-gray-900`,
  },
  highlight: {
    container: `${base.container} border-blue-500 bg-blue-50`,
    title: `${base.title} text-blue-900`,
    description: `${base.description} text-blue-800`,
    cta: `${base.cta} text-blue-700`,
  },
  muted: {
    container: `${base.container} border-gray-200 bg-gray-50`,
    title: `${base.title} text-gray-700`,
    description: `${base.description} text-gray-600`,
    cta: `${base.cta} text-gray-600`,
  },
};

const getStyles = (variant: Variant): StyleGroup => {
  return styles[variant] ?? styles.default;
};
```

## Notes

- Prefer object maps when variants are mostly static and easy to enumerate.
- Prefer `switch` when variant logic needs branching, composition, or computed conditions.
- Prefer colocated style files when style logic adds noise to component rendering logic.
